Model formulation

We used a generalized linear mixed-effect model⁴¹ with DLNM⁴². We
adjusted for the effects of relative humidity on the day of exposure to
determine whether the iCFR was affected by it on the days when the
indexed cases were exposed. As the iCFR represents the probability of
death following infection, we modeled the number of deaths out of the
total number of cases. A binomial distribution is an appropriate
approach for modeling this type of outcome, where the variable of
interest is the number of successes (i.e. deaths) out of a fixed number
of trials (i.e. cases)⁴³. Therefore, we employed a logistic regression
model under the Distributed Lag Non-Linear Models (DLNM) framework,
which is well-suited for analyzing non-linear relationships between
exposure and outcome variables over time.

